{"kind":"request","text":"Finish the MCP boundary beside NF-1: move the bounded tool description to `packages/mcp`, and tie the act output schema to one typed result.\n\nFiled 2026-11-11 21:14 EST. Hugh asked for this work on 11 November and said to do it as soon as possible. Every fact below is read at main `df41e599`. This request follows the neutral `Form` request `1a9330c2`, which the planner placed as NF-1 (`6050f6ea`). It asks the planner to place this work as the next head after NF-1, in the same lane.\n\nA separate head is cleaner than a wider NF-1. NF-1's conditions say that `docs/mcp-tools.json` changes only at the two `time` schemas. Part 2 below changes the act output schema, which appears 59 times in that file.\n\n## The problem\n\n1. **The bounded tool description lives in the client.** `packages/client/src/describe.ts` holds `DESCRIPTION_CHARS`, the 1,000-character bound on an MCP tool description. It also holds `Described` and `describeAct`, which cut an act's text to that bound and add a pointer to `inspect`. Their one consumer is `describedOf` in `packages/mcp/src/catalogue.ts`.\n2. **A shim hides the direction.** `packages/mcp/src/describe.ts` exports the client's names again under the comment \"retain the existing MCP module exports\". `packages/mcp/src/shape.ts:159` does the same for `expectedOf`, `viaLeft`, `effectiveFields` and `finalStates`. `apps/views` then imports `ActLike`, `effectiveFields` and `expectedOf` from `@generalbusiness/dap-mcp`, although the client owns them.\n3. **Two functions project an `Answer`, and neither is typed.** `#answerOf` at `packages/mcp/src/service.ts:901` builds the result of an act tool. `projectAnswer` at `service.ts:192` builds the result of a step and of an install. Both return `Record\u003cstring, unknown\u003e`. Only `projectAnswer` adds `effects`. Only `#answerOf` adds `phase`.\n4. **`ACT_OUTPUT` is written by hand and has drifted.** `packages/mcp/src/catalogue.ts:224` lists `answer`, `sentence`, `next`, `scope`, `kind`, `tool`, `fact`, `outboxIds`, `reason`, `name`, `judgedAt` and `retained`. `#answerOf` also emits `archived` and `phase`. The description of `answer` names five values and omits `archived`. The schema is open, so no host refuses a result today. A host that reads the schema does not learn that an act can be archived.\n5. **The server reads its own results back by string key.** `structuredContent[` appears at `service.ts:496`, `552` and `553`, and on five lines of `packages/mcp/src/views/session.ts` (351, 378, 448, 452 and 454). Line 553 casts `fact` to `{ seq: number }`. The typed `Answer` was in hand a few calls earlier.\n6. **Not audited.** `service.ts` writes the input schema of each fixed tool by hand, beside its parser. Nobody has compared them. NF-1 found one mismatch of this kind in `time`.\n\n## The work\n\nOne head, three parts.\n\n1. **Move the bound.** Move `DESCRIPTION_CHARS`, `Described` and `describeAct` to `packages/mcp`. Delete the re-exports in `packages/mcp/src/describe.ts` and at `shape.ts:159`. Each importer takes a name from the package that owns it. The sentences, the field lines, the guard sentences and `describeActLine` stay in the client. They are neutral words, and a page or a foldkit adapter can use them.\n2. **One typed act result.** Declare one result type in `packages/mcp` for an act, a step and an install. One function builds it from an `Answer`. `ACT_OUTPUT` lists every member, and a `satisfies` clause keyed by the members of the type fails the build when the two differ. The description of `answer` names every value. The in-process callers at the eight sites above read the typed result, not `structuredContent`.\n3. **Audit the fixed tools.** For each tool that `service.ts` declares with a hand-written input schema, compare the schema with the parser. Report a table: tool, field, what the schema admits, what the parser admits. Fix in this head only a schema that admits a value the parser refuses. List every other difference for the planner.\n\n## Rulings the planner owes with the schedule\n\n- **`effects`.** A step result carries a count of effects and an act result does not. Rule whether the one result type carries it for all three.\n- **`phase` in the schema.** `phase` is custody's word. Rule whether it stays in the act result or moves under a named member.\n\n## What to weigh\n\n- **Breaking change.** `DESCRIPTION_CHARS`, `Described` and one member of `descriptionsOf` leave `@generalbusiness/dap-client`. Several names leave `@generalbusiness/dap-mcp`. The stable base is the last cheap moment, as for NF-1.\n- **Collision.** The head edits `packages/mcp/src/service.ts` and `catalogue.ts`. Order it after S4, after the cutover head and after NF-1.\n- **Tool list bytes.** Part 2 adds members to an output schema that appears 59 times in `docs/mcp-tools.json`. Report the change in bytes against the MCP budget. A shared `$defs` entry is one way to hold the cost, if the four hosts accept it.\n- **Kernel.** No kernel file changes and no record form is added.\n- **Deployment.** The hosted MCP Workers carry the change. `MCP_ENABLED` stays off until S4 lands.\n- **Size.** An estimate from reading, not a measurement: about 45 lines move from `describe.ts`, and the two projections are about 20 lines together. The audit in part 3 is the open cost.","body":{"conditions":"packages/client holds no DESCRIPTION_CHARS, Described or describeAct. packages/mcp exports again no name that packages/client owns. One typed result in packages/mcp serves an act, a step and an install, and ACT_OUTPUT fails the build when it differs from that type. No in-process caller reads structuredContent by string key. The audit table of fixed tool schemas against their parsers is reported. docs/mcp-tools.json changes only at the act output schema and at audited fixes, with the change in bytes reported. npm run gate passes.","target_head":"df41e59964f09ce1d723ce870f5e4e5c6d8d4641","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}